Chapter 5: Results and Evaluation

5.1 Introduction

This chapter presents the experimental findings from the phishing detection study. The results are reported separately for each dataset, each classifier and each imbalance treatment method to support transparent comparison and to enable detailed analysis of how different configurations behave under class imbalance. The analysis focuses on the performance of the models when applied to skewed binary classification tasks, using metrics that are appropriate for imbalanced datasets rather than relying on overall accuracy alone.

The structure of this chapter follows the experimental design established in Chapter 4. First, results are presented by dataset to assess whether performance patterns are consistent across different feature representations. Second, classifiers are compared to determine which learning paradigm is most robust under class imbalance. Third, imbalance treatment methods are evaluated to identify which resampling or weighting strategies provide the strongest improvement in minority-class detection. Fourth, a best-versus-worst analysis highlights the practical impact of configuration choices on model behaviour.

The chapter also includes a best-versus-worst analysis to highlight the practical effect of different balancing strategies. This is important because raw averages alone do not always reveal why one configuration outperforms another, especially when trade-offs exist between precision and recall. In addition, the chapter reports the outcomes of statistical significance tests to confirm whether observed performance differences are meaningful rather than due to sampling variation. This approach ensures that the findings are not only descriptive but also statistically defensible.

All experiments were conducted using the methodology and evaluation framework described in Chapter 4. Performance is measured using precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC and Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC). These metrics were selected because they provide a comprehensive view of classifier behaviour on imbalanced data, with particular emphasis on the minority phishing class, which is the main focus of detection systems in practice.

5.2 Results by Dataset

Results are first reported separately for each dataset to assess whether the imbalance treatment techniques and classifiers perform consistently across different feature representations. This section presents the performance of all classifier and imbalance-method combinations on the UCI Phishing Websites dataset and the Hannousse and Yahiouche benchmark dataset. Presenting the findings by dataset makes it easier to identify whether a method performs well in both benchmark settings or only under one particular feature structure.

