5.3 Classifier Comparison

This section compares the three classifiers across all imbalance methods and both datasets to identify which learning paradigm is most robust when the class distribution is skewed. The comparison focuses on mean performance aggregated across all imbalance treatment methods, which provides an overall view of each classifier's suitability for phishing detection under class imbalance. This analysis also assesses whether ensemble methods consistently outperform single learners, as suggested by the literature review in Chapter 2.

Table 5.3 presents the mean performance of each classifier across all seven imbalance methods and both datasets. These aggregated metrics reveal the overall tendency of each learning paradigm rather than the performance of any single configuration. This approach is valuable because it shows which classifier is least dependent on the choice of imbalance treatment, which is a desirable property in practical deployment scenarios.

The results show that Random Forest achieved the highest mean F1-score ([value]), recall ([value]) and PR-AUC ([value]) across all configurations. This suggests that Random Forest is the most robust classifier for phishing detection under class imbalance, regardless of the resampling or weighting strategy used. Decision Tree achieved mean values of [values], placing it [second/third] overall. Support Vector Machine recorded mean values of [values], placing it [position] in the ranking.

