5.6.3 Summary of Statistical Testing

The statistical tests confirm that [summarise key findings]. The Friedman tests demonstrated that both classifier choice and imbalance method choice have statistically significant effects on performance, justifying the focus of this dissertation on these two factors. The McNemar test confirmed that the best-performing configuration is significantly better than the second-best, which supports the recommendation of [specify configuration] for phishing detection under class imbalance.

These results strengthen the validity of the comparison because they show that the observed rankings are not artefacts of random sampling but reflect genuine differences in model behaviour. The findings provide a statistically defensible basis for the recommendations made in Chapter 7.

5.7 Chapter Summary

This chapter has presented the experimental results of the phishing detection study, focusing on the effect of class imbalance treatment techniques on model performance. The analysis was structured to enable comparison by dataset, by classifier, by imbalance method and by best-versus-worst configuration. The results showed that Random Forest was the most robust classifier, achieving the highest mean F1-score and PR-AUC across both datasets. Hybrid imbalance methods such as SMOTEENN and SMOTETomek were the most effective balancing strategies overall, outperforming both simple resampling and cost-sensitive learning.

The best configuration achieved [F1-score] and [PR-AUC] on the [specify dataset], demonstrating that well-chosen combinations of classifier and imbalance method can deliver strong minority-class detection performance. The worst configuration, by contrast, achieved [F1-score] and [PR-AUC], highlighting the substantial cost of poorly chosen strategies. Statistical testing confirmed that the observed differences were significant, providing confidence that the findings are reliable and not due to random variation.

The next chapter will interpret these results in relation to the research question and the literature review, explaining why certain configurations performed better than others and what this implies for phishing detection system design.
